     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg per day, which is twice the usual maximum daily dose of ibuprofen for adults (3,200 mg/day). A dose that high greatly raises the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function. It looks like a possible error, such as 1600 mg per day or 400 mg four times daily.\n\nTypical adult oral dosing is:\n- **400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours as needed**, up to 3,200 mg/day. This is the usual range for pain or inflammation.\n- **1,200 mg/day** or less for OTC use.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n1. If the intended dose was different, for example 800 mg four times daily (3,200 mg/day) or 400 mg four times daily, tell me and I'll enter it.\n2. If a prescriber really intends 1600 mg four times daily, they should confirm it directly. The order would also need pharmacy review.\n3. Because a medication order needs a licensed prescriber's judgment, the treating clinician should place or confirm it.\n\nWhich would you like to do?"
      }
